# core/discover-predicates - Which predicates are in use, and on what?
#
# Answers: every predicate in the chosen graph role, its frequency, and whether
# its objects are IRIs or literals.
#
# Does NOT prove: that a predicate means what its local name suggests. Read the
# published ontology for that.
#
# Two uses. First, finding the property that holds something the profile has no
# role for - a house field promoted through the converter's extension route lands
# here and nowhere else. Second, catching near-duplicate spellings: two casings of
# the same key are two predicates, and this is where that shows up as two rows
# with suspiciously split counts.
#
# ?objectKinds is every kind this predicate's objects take, not a sample of one.
# Two independent SAMPLE aggregates pick independently, so a kind sampled from one
# solution and an object sampled from another could describe a pair that never
# occurred - and the incoherence is invisible, because both values are real. Listing
# the kinds is also the more useful answer: a predicate carrying both IRIs and
# literals is worth seeing, and a single sample hid exactly that.
#
# ?exampleObject remains a sample, which is what it says it is: one object drawn from
# the solutions, with no claim to be representative.
#
# Parameters: LIMIT
{{PREFIXES}}
SELECT ?predicate (COUNT(*) AS ?uses)
       (GROUP_CONCAT(DISTINCT ?kind; separator=", ") AS ?objectKinds)
       (SAMPLE(STR(?object)) AS ?exampleObject)
WHERE {
  {{GRAPH_OPEN:semantic}}
    ?s ?predicate ?object .
    BIND(IF(isIRI(?object), "iri", IF(isBlank(?object), "blank", "literal")) AS ?kind)
  {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
}
GROUP BY ?predicate
ORDER BY DESC(?uses) ?predicate
LIMIT {{LIMIT}}
